---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const layers = `/* declared once, first, in every dist file: */
@layer bo-reset, bo-tokens, bo-primitives, bo-components, bo-utilities;

/* YOUR css — written outside any layer — always wins: */
.bo-btn { border-radius: 0; }   /* beats the component layer, no !important */`;
---
<Gallery title="The cascade contract">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Every framework rule lives inside a named <code>@layer</code>. Unlayered CSS
    beats all layered CSS regardless of specificity — so <strong>your stylesheet is
    the escape hatch</strong>, by design, forever. No specificity wars, no
    <code>!important</code>, and leaving the framework is cheap: your overrides
    already outrank it.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The order</h2>
    <pre><code>{layers}</code></pre>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>reset → tokens → primitives → components → utilities</strong>: utilities win over components (the one Tailwind-ism kept — a small curated set, not a system).</li>
      <li>The layer-order statement is byte-identical in every dist file, and re-declaring an existing order is a no-op — any à-la-carte import combination yields the same cascade.</li>
      <li>Re-branding = remapping the semantic token tier in your unlayered file; see <a href={base + '/concepts/theming'}>Theming</a>. Never override component classes for brand.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>What this buys at screen #200</h2>
    <p>Overrides stay one-liners instead of specificity ladders; framework upgrades
    can't silently outrank app CSS (layered can never beat unlayered); and audits are
    mechanical — anything inside <code>@layer bo-*</code> is ours, anything outside
    is yours.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">One sharp edge: an unlayered <em>reset you didn't
    write</em> (Tailwind v3 preflight, normalize) wins by the same rule and strips
    components — wrap it in a lower layer; recipe + live proof in
    <a href={base + '/getting-started/troubleshooting'}>Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/concepts/theming", "Theming"], ["/concepts/tokens", "Design tokens"], ["/getting-started/troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting (reset interop)"]]} />
</Gallery>
